  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nThe remnants of a MCS are moving east across the area early this \nafternoon. Some low and mid level clouds have been observed along \nwith a brief wind shift. While the old MCS itself will not produce \nany rainfall, subtle areas of convergence and differential heating \ncould lead to storm development later this afternoon when the PBL \ndestabilizes. Locally heavy rain and gusty winds are the main threat \nwith any isolated storms this afternoon in the moist environment. \n\nLater this evening, another decaying MCS moves in from the northwest \nwell ahead of an approaching cold front. Showers and storms are once \nagain possible, especially along and northwest of I-71. There does \nappear to be at least some damaging wind potential if the MCS holds \ntogether due to increasing shear and high DCAPE values across \neastern Indiana and western Ohio this evening/overnight. \n\nIn terms of heat, some cloud cover has initially limited temperatures \ntoday as the cloud debris from an old MCS has moved overhead. These \nclouds progress to the east this afternoon and should give way to \nsome more sunshine. Combined temperatures in the upper 80s with dew \npoints in the lower 70s should support heat index values in the \nupper 90s to near 100 well into the afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nA cold front associated with a low in Canada pushes east through the \nregion on Friday morning. Drier and slightly cooler air moves into \nthe Ohio Valley behind the front. High pressure progresses across \nthe area on Saturday bringing dry conditions and near average \ntemperatures. \n\nShower and storm chances increase once again on Sunday when another \ncold front associated with a low far to the north in Canada moves \nthrough. There could be a window for severe weather as some models \nare depicting instability overlapping with increasing shear ahead of \nthe front during the day on Sunday. The best potential for storms \nappears to be in the southeast where frontal passage occurs later in \nthe day. \n\nCooler and drier air arrives for the start of next week behind the \nfront.",
